"# Appendix B — The Honest Miss

Appendix A taught you to *read* an error: the clean errors, the leaky
ones, and the census card behind a count. This appendix teaches the
opposite skill — reading what the census *cannot* show you. It is the
hardest honesty a metric can offer: knowing where its own vision ends.

The error census is a WRONG SINK. Every failure that escapes a running
program drains into it, is normalized to a stable key, and is counted.
That is a real and useful thing. But a sink only collects what flows
*into* it, and the census sits at exactly one place in the pipeline:
the run / eval / REPL boundary. `noteError` in `lib/system/verb-stats.js`
fires only for errors that ESCAPE a program at that boundary. Anything
that fails somewhere else — earlier, quieter, or not at all — never
reaches the drain.

So there are three blind spots the census cannot see *by construction*.
Not bugs. Not gaps to be patched by reading harder. Structural silences.
This appendix names all three, and then draws the one lesson that ties
them together: a zero in this census means \"unmet here, so far,\" never
\"impossible.\"

## B.1 Blind spot (a): the silent non-error

The most dangerous failures make no noise at all. Motoi's core is
permissive — it coerces rather than complains. So a whole class of
programs are *wrong* and yet throw *nothing*:

```scheme
(display (string-length 5))
;; => 1
```

That should be a type error — `5` is not a string. Instead the host
coerces `5` to `\"5\"`, measures it, and hands back `1`. No `error:`
line. No condition raised. `guard` never fires. And because nothing was
thrown, the census counted *nothing*. Same shape here:

```scheme
(display (/ 1 0))
;; => +inf.0
```

Division by zero is IEEE floating point in Motoi, not a raised error. It
yields `+inf.0` and the program runs to the end. The census stays flat.

These are not errors — they are wrong *answers*. A sink for thrown
wrongs cannot catch a wrong that was never thrown. That is blind spot
(a), and it is why the disciplined reflex is not \"read the error\" but
\"check the result.\" The full catalogue of observed silent non-errors —
`(+ 1 \"x\")`, `(cons 1 2 3)`, `(sqrt -4)`, arity mismatches, out-of-range
reads — lives in the error card
`scheme/reference/error-cards/silent-non-errors.slatl`. Read it when a
result looks wrong but the census is empty; the emptiness is the census
being honest, not the program being fine.

## B.2 Blind spot (b): reader-time and expand-time errors

The census watches the *eval* boundary. But a program has to be READ
into forms, and macros have to be EXPANDED into core forms, before
`eval` ever runs a line. Errors in those two upstream phases are real,
they stop your program, they print a clear message — and they fire
*before* the hook that counts:

```scheme
(display \"abc
;; error: unterminated string (line 1, col 10)
```

That is a genuine failure. Your program did not run. But it failed in
the READER, turning source text into forms, upstream of `eval`. The
census never saw it. The same is true of a missing paren
(`error: missing )`), an unexpected close paren, an end-of-input
mid-form, and a runaway macro that expands into itself. All real, all
common in language and module work, all invisible to `stats/error-*`.

There is a sharp consequence for detection. You cannot `guard` a reader
error inside the same source that fails to read — the whole text is
rejected before evaluation begins, so there is nothing for `guard` to
wrap:

```scheme
(display (guard (e (#t 'caught)) 1
;; error: missing ) (line 1, col 10)
```

The `guard` you wrote is never reached; the reader error escapes past
it. Detection here is a TOOLING job — editor paren-balance, string-close
checks, a base case on every recursive macro — not a runtime one. The
full accounting, including the honest note that several real reader
messages do not even match their intended roster family regex, is in
`scheme/reference/error-cards/expand-and-reader-time.slatl`. The lesson
for this book: **a flat census after a parse failure is not 'no
problems' — it is the census looking the other way.**

## B.3 Blind spot (c): reserved keys that never fire

Open `_ERR_FAMILIES` in `lib/system/verb-stats.js` and you will find
fourteen family regexes — reserved coordinates for classic error kinds.
Reserving a coordinate is not the same as occupying it. Several of those
keys have no code path in Motoi that throws a message matching them.
They sit at zero, forever, until the language changes.

You can ask the census directly:

```scheme
(stats/error-count-of \"division-by-zero\")
;; => 0

(stats/error-count-of \"not-a-pair\")
;; => 0
```

Both are zero — and will stay zero — because the underlying operations
are *silent non-errors* today (`(/ 1 0)` returns `+inf.0`; `(car 5)`
returns nothing) rather than clean throws. The key `division-by-zero` is
a *promise the taxonomy makes*, not a *description of what Motoi does*.
The reserved roster (`not-a-pair`, `wrong-arity`, `division-by-zero`,
`index-out-of-range`, `program-too-big`, `frozen-sandbox`,
`unquote-splicing-misplaced`) is a to-do list for making the language
stricter, catalogued honestly in
`scheme/reference/error-cards/roster-not-observed.slatl`. When one of
these starts throwing cleanly at the eval boundary, its count will begin
to rise and the reserved coordinate graduates into an observed one.

## B.4 The one lesson: a zero is 'unmet here, so far'

Put the three blind spots together and a single discipline falls out.
When `stats/error-count-of` returns `0`, it can mean any of at least
four different things:

1. This wrong genuinely never happened. (The optimistic reading.)
2. This wrong happens silently — no throw, so nothing to count (B.1).
3. This wrong fires before the eval boundary — read/expand time (B.2).
4. This key is reserved but no code path throws it yet (B.3).

The census cannot tell these apart for you. It reports the count and
nothing more. So the honest reading of a zero is never \"this is
impossible\" — it is **\"unmet here, so far.\"** Unmet: no escaped throw
landed on this key. Here: at the eval boundary, which is not the whole
pipeline. So far: over the sessions on disk, which is not all time.

This is the deepest limit in the whole book, because it is a limit *of a
measurement*, not of the language. A programmer — or a small model
writing code — who reads a zero as proof of safety will ship the exact
bug the census could not see. The operator who reads a zero as \"unmet
here, so far\" reaches past the metric: checks the result for a silent
coercion, balances the parens the reader would have choked on, and does
not claim \"Motoi throws division-by-zero\" when it actually returns
`+inf.0`.

## B.5 The reflex, stated once

- A nonzero count is a *fact*: that wrong escaped, that many times, at
  the eval boundary. Trust it as far as it goes.
- A zero count is *not* a fact about the world — it is a fact about the
  sink. Ask which of the four readings applies before you rely on it.
- When correctness matters and the census is silent, add the throw the
  language does not: guard your own preconditions with `(error ...)` so
  a silent wrong becomes a loud, countable one — moving it out of the
  blind spot and into the sink where it can be seen.
- For reader and macro failures, reach for tooling, not telemetry. The
  census will stay flat; that is correct, not comforting.

Reasoning about what a metric CANNOT see is the same skill as reasoning
about what a *language* cannot do — the skill this whole book is about.
The census is honest precisely because it does not pretend its silence
is safety. Neither should you.
"
)
